WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.103 --> 00:00:11.843
It looked like a good plan at the time.

2
00:00:12.744 --> 00:00:17.865
We were down South, in Alabama—Bill Driscoll and me—when a kidnapping idea struck us.

3
00:00:18.665 --> 00:00:21.886
Afterward, Bill called the plan "temporary insanity."

4
00:00:22.587 --> 00:00:27.008
There was a town, as flat as a flapjack, and it was called Summit, of course.

5
00:00:27.976 --> 00:00:32.858
A bunch of country hicks lived there, unremarkable folks as harmless as they were full of themselves.

6
00:00:33.598 --> 00:00:37.419
Bill and I had about six hundred dollars, and we needed two thousand more to pull off a

7
00:00:37.559 --> 00:00:39.299
sweet get-rich-quick scheme.

8
00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:43.401
With that money, we were going to sell some land out in western Illinois.

9
00:00:44.341 --> 00:00:47.962
Of course, it wasn't our land to sell, but that was the beauty of it!

10
00:00:47.982 --> 00:00:53.044
"A kidnapping job could work perfectly in this town," said Bill as we talked on the steps of

11
00:00:53.064 --> 00:00:53.824
the Summit Hotel.

12
00:00:54.964 --> 00:00:58.826
"In a big city, the newspapers would get a hold of it and stir things up with nosy

13
00:00:58.866 --> 00:01:03.728
reporters. But out here, Sam, there's nothing but philoprogenitiveness for miles!"

14
00:01:03.748 --> 00:01:05.808
"Philo—what?" I asked.

15
00:01:05.828 --> 00:01:07.949
"Philoprogenitiveness," he said.

16
00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:10.250
I looked at him like he was crazy.

17
00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:13.091
"It means loving one's children," he said.

18
00:01:13.111 --> 00:01:15.852
"Parents here will do anything for their kids.

19
00:01:16.653 --> 00:01:21.014
And with Summit being such a small town, the law can't come after us with anything better than

20
00:01:21.034 --> 00:01:23.375
an old sheriff and his dog."

21
00:01:23.395 --> 00:01:24.596
"Sounds good," I said.

22
00:01:25.248 --> 00:01:30.131
We selected our victim, the only child of a well-known citizen named Ebenezer Dorset.

23
00:01:30.851 --> 00:01:34.394
The father was a banker, respectable but tight with his wallet.

24
00:01:35.314 --> 00:01:37.295
"He's a stern, decent type," said Bill.

25
00:01:37.696 --> 00:01:41.018
"The kind that goes to church every Sunday and passes the collection plate.

26
00:01:41.778 --> 00:01:43.159
Without adding to it, of course."

27
00:01:43.979 --> 00:01:44.560
I shrugged.

28
00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:47.722
"At least he doesn't subtract from it."

29
00:01:48.142 --> 00:01:48.862
"Right you are!

30
00:01:49.163 --> 00:01:50.523
So that means he has money!"

31
00:01:50.683 --> 00:01:51.324
laughed Bill.

32
00:01:52.192 --> 00:01:56.836
The banker's son was a boy of ten, with dark freckles and hair the color of red pepper.

33
00:01:57.756 --> 00:02:02.140
It wouldn't take much for rich old Ebenezer to melt down and pay a ransom of two thousand

34
00:02:02.180 --> 00:02:04.942
dollars. That's what we figured, anyway.

35
00:02:05.683 --> 00:02:10.466
Two miles from Summit, there was a little mountain covered with shrubby cedar, which had a cave on

36
00:02:10.606 --> 00:02:11.127
one side.

37
00:02:12.007 --> 00:02:15.650
We secretly stored food and other items we'd need for the kidnapping in there.

38
00:02:16.731 --> 00:02:21.351
As soon as we were ready, we drove in a rented buggy past old Dorset's house one evening

39
00:02:21.451 --> 00:02:22.732
just after sundown.

40
00:02:23.652 --> 00:02:27.215
The banker's kid was outside, throwing rocks at a neighbor's kitten.

41
00:02:27.995 --> 00:02:28.896
"Hey, little boy!"

42
00:02:29.176 --> 00:02:30.617
said Bill, smiling sweetly.

43
00:02:30.637 --> 00:02:33.760
"Want some candy and a nice buggy ride?"

44
00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:37.702
Suddenly, the boy caught Bill neatly in the eye with a piece of brick.

45
00:02:39.163 --> 00:02:45.168
"Ow! That'll cost the old man an extra five hundred dollars," muttered Bill, getting out of the buggy.

46
00:02:46.112 --> 00:02:50.436
The boy wrestled like an angry bear, so we held him down in the bottom of the buggy

47
00:02:50.516 --> 00:02:51.777
and drove away quickly.

48
00:02:52.617 --> 00:02:55.280
We got to the cave and I hitched the horse to a tree.

49
00:02:56.200 --> 00:03:00.964
After dark, I drove back to Summit, returned the rented buggy, and walked back to the mountain.

50
00:03:02.045 --> 00:03:06.429
When I arrived, Bill was holding a wet rag on his black eye from the piece of brick.

51
00:03:07.289 --> 00:03:10.152
A fire burned behind the big rock at the entrance of the cave.

52
00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:15.882
The boy was sitting in front of a pot of boiling coffee, and he had two wild-turkey feathers

53
00:03:15.942 --> 00:03:17.002
stuck in his red hair.

54
00:03:18.003 --> 00:03:20.224
He pointed a sharpened stick at me and said, "Ha!

55
00:03:20.844 --> 00:03:25.866
Cursed paleface, do you dare enter the camp of Red Chief, the terror of the plains?"

56
00:03:26.987 --> 00:03:30.268
Bill rolled up his trousers and showed me his various shin wounds.

57
00:03:31.028 --> 00:03:32.309
"That kid kicks hard.

58
00:03:32.629 --> 00:03:33.629
But he's calm now.

59
00:03:34.169 --> 00:03:36.030
We were playing cowboys and Indians.

60
00:03:37.131 --> 00:03:40.072
I'm Old Hank the horse thief, Red Chief's captive.

61
00:03:40.532 --> 00:03:42.293
I'm to be hanged on Saturday."

62
00:03:43.754 --> 00:03:45.355
The boy was having a great time.

63
00:03:46.335 --> 00:03:49.997
Camping in a cave was so much fun that he forgot he was the captive.

64
00:03:51.138 --> 00:03:54.440
"You're Snake-eye, the spy," the boy said, pointing at me.

65
00:03:54.460 --> 00:03:56.501
"Snake-eye, eh?"

66
00:03:56.861 --> 00:03:57.301
I said.

67
00:03:57.321 --> 00:03:59.542
"Nice name, don't you think, Bill?"

68
00:04:00.523 --> 00:04:02.884
Bill held the rag over his eye and didn't answer.

69
00:04:03.685 --> 00:04:05.366
The boy stabbed his stick in the dirt.

70
00:04:05.386 --> 00:04:11.180
"When my braves return from the warpath, you will be broiled at the stake," he said all matter-of-fact,

71
00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:13.421
like burning folks up happened every day.

72
00:04:14.541 --> 00:04:15.342
We ate supper.

73
00:04:15.742 --> 00:04:20.223
The kid filled his mouth with bacon, bread, and gravy, then began to talk.

74
00:04:20.983 --> 00:04:22.864
He didn't stop, even to breathe.

75
00:04:22.884 --> 00:04:25.064
"I like this cave fine.

76
00:04:25.724 --> 00:04:26.585
I hate school.

77
00:04:27.265 --> 00:04:29.906
Rats ate sixteen of Jimmy Talbot's hen's eggs.

78
00:04:31.006 --> 00:04:32.986
Are there any real Indians in these woods?

79
00:04:33.407 --> 00:04:34.407
I want more gravy.

80
00:04:35.227 --> 00:04:36.687
Do trees make the wind blow?

81
00:04:37.068 --> 00:04:38.208
We have five puppies.

82
00:04:39.104 --> 00:04:40.785
What makes your nose so red, Hank?

83
00:04:41.365 --> 00:04:42.685
My father has lots of money.

84
00:04:43.725 --> 00:04:44.546
Are stars hot?

85
00:04:45.086 --> 00:04:46.866
I whipped Ed Walker twice, Saturday.

86
00:04:47.366 --> 00:04:48.247
I don't like girls.

87
00:04:48.707 --> 00:04:49.667
Do cows talk?

88
00:04:50.687 --> 00:04:52.008
Why are oranges round?

89
00:04:52.548 --> 00:04:54.429
Are there beds to sleep on in this cave?

90
00:04:55.029 --> 00:04:56.589
Amos Murray has six toes.

91
00:04:57.629 --> 00:04:59.970
A parrot can talk, but a monkey or a fish can't.

92
00:05:00.410 --> 00:05:02.051
How many does it take to make twelve?"

93
00:05:03.031 --> 00:05:05.972
Every few minutes, the boy would remember who he was supposed to be.

94
00:05:06.816 --> 00:05:10.217
He'd grab his spear and tiptoe to the mouth of the cave to hunt for enemies.

95
00:05:11.118 --> 00:05:36.933
Then out of the blue, he'd let loose with a blood-chilling war whoop: "AAAHHH-OOOUUU!"

96
00:05:36.953 --> 00:05:39.475
Terrified, Bill dropped his dinner plate and started shivering so hard, the beans jumped off his lap.

97
00:05:39.495 --> 00:05:40.456
"Red Chief," I said, "do you want to go home?"

98
00:05:40.516 --> 00:05:40.977
"Aw, do I have to?"

99
00:05:41.037 --> 00:05:41.277
he said.

100
00:05:41.297 --> 00:05:41.877
"I can't have fun at home.

101
00:05:41.917 --> 00:05:42.338
I like camping out.

102
00:05:42.358 --> 00:05:43.539
You won't take me back, Snake-eye, will you?"

103
00:05:43.579 --> 00:05:44.360
"Not right away," I said.

104
00:05:44.380 --> 00:05:45.541
"We'll stay here for a while."

105
00:05:45.601 --> 00:05:46.002
"All right!"

106
00:05:46.062 --> 00:05:46.282
he said.

107
00:05:46.302 --> 00:05:47.703
"I've never had so much fun."

108
00:05:47.743 --> 00:05:48.324
We got to bed late.

109
00:05:48.864 --> 00:05:51.927
We spread some blankets and quilts and put Red Chief between us.

110
00:05:53.129 --> 00:05:54.570
But we weren't afraid he'd run away.

111
00:05:54.610 --> 00:06:00.015
Heck, he kept us awake for three hours, jumping up, grabbing his stick, and screeching right into our

112
00:06:00.115 --> 00:06:05.284
ears. Every time a leaf rustled, he'd run to the cave entrance and shout to an imaginary band

113
00:06:05.304 --> 00:06:07.746
of outlaws, "You can't sneak up on us!"

114
00:06:08.747 --> 00:06:10.809
At last I fell into a troubled sleep.

115
00:06:11.829 --> 00:06:17.554
I dreamed I'd been kidnapped and chained to a tree by a short, fast-talking pirate with bright red

116
00:06:17.594 --> 00:06:17.835
hair.
